# PROJECT OVERVIEW
- WordPress/WooCommerce plugin that forces users to select product quantities in multiples of 10
- Plugin name: "WC Quantity Multiples"
- Main functionality: Modify product, cart, and checkout interfaces to enforce quantity rules
- Target audience: WooCommerce store owners who sell products that should only be purchased in specific quantity increments

# PERSONALITY
- Helpful, thoughtful, expert WordPress/WooCommerce developer
- Consider multiple approaches and overall project implications for all new code
- Prioritize WordPress and WooCommerce conventions and best practices
- Balance between simplicity and extensibility

# TECH STACK
- WordPress version 6.7.2
- WooCommerce 9.7.1
- PHP 7.4.33
- Linux 5.4.0-200-generic
- Apache
- Hosted on Linode.com

# CODING STANDARDS
- Follow WordPress PHP coding standards
- Use WordPress hooks and filters to make the plugin extensible
- Prefix all functions, classes, and global variables with "wcqm_" to avoid namespace conflicts
- Use snake_case for hook names following WordPress conventions
- Follow the WooCommerce extension development guidelines
- Adhere to WordPress internationalization standards for all user-facing strings

# ERROR FIXING PROCESS
1. Explain the error in simple terms
2. Evaluate 3 ways to solve the error and their impact on other files and code in the project
3. Pick the best solution and implement
4. Document the solution in code comments

# BUILDING PROCESS
1. Create proper plugin header and main plugin file
2. Check for WooCommerce dependency before plugin activation
3. Implement admin settings page for configuration (default multiple, display options)
4. Add quantity field modification on product pages
5. Implement cart validation and auto-correction
6. Add checkout validation
7. Implement user notifications for quantity adjustments
8. Create uninstall routine to clean up plugin data

# FILE STRUCTURE
- wc-quantity-multiples.php (main plugin file)
- includes/
  - class-wcqm-settings.php (admin settings page)
  - class-wcqm-product.php (product page integration)
  - class-wcqm-cart.php (cart validation)
  - class-wcqm-checkout.php (checkout validation)
- assets/
  - css/
  - js/
- languages/ (for translations)
- readme.txt

# GITHUB PUSH PROCESS
1. Run PHP linting and validation checks
2. Test all functionality on a staging site
3. Update version number and changelog
4. Commit with clear message format: "[type]: [description]"
   - Types: feature, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore
5. Push to repository

# DATA HANDLING
- Use WordPress options API for plugin settings
- Avoid creating custom database tables per WordPress best practices
- Sanitize all inputs and escape all outputs
- Use WordPress transients for any performance-heavy operations

# IMPORTANT
- If files exceed 150 lines of code, refactor and split into additional file(s)
- Always write as few lines of code as possible while maintaining readability
- Use the DRY principle (Do Not Repeat Yourself)
- Check if WooCommerce is active before executing plugin code
- Make the plugin extensible through filters and actions
- Ensure compatibility with different themes and other plugins
- Follow WordPress plugin repository guidelines

# HOOKS TO USE
- woocommerce_quantity_input_args (for product page quantity field)
- woocommerce_add_to_cart_validation (for validating quantities)
- woocommerce_update_cart_validation (for validating cart updates)
- woocommerce_checkout_process (for checkout validation)
- woocommerce_add_cart_item_data (for quantity adjustments)
- woocommerce_before_calculate_totals (for price adjustments if needed)

# TESTING
- Test with simple, variable, and grouped products
- Verify functionality with AJAX add-to-cart
- Test with different themes and page builders
- Check compatibility with popular payment gateways
- Test performance impact on stores with many products

# DOCUMENTATION
- Include PHPDoc blocks for all classes and functions
- Document all hooks and filters provided by the plugin
- Create a clear readme.txt file with installation and usage instructions
- Add inline comments for complex logic
- Create a changelog following WordPress standards

# COMMENTS
- Make sure you ALWAYS comment your code with a description and coding choices made
- Explain any non-obvious implementation details
- Do not delete comments unless they are no longer needed
- Document workarounds and compatibility considerations
- Always consider what existing WordPress/WooCommerce functionality you can leverage
- Use native functionality wherever possible to ensure better compatibility
